  },
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Contour Integrals and Cauchy's Theorem [S+N]\n\nA **contour** in $\\mathbb{C}$ is a piecewise-$C^1$ path $\\gamma : [a, b] \\to \\mathbb{C}$. The **contour integral** of a continuous $f : \\gamma \\to \\mathbb{C}$ is\n\n$$\\int_\\gamma f(z)\\,dz = \\int_a^b f(\\gamma(t))\\,\\gamma'(t)\\,dt.$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as the line integral of a vector field over $\\gamma \\subset \\mathbb{R}^2$ — the multiplication is **complex multiplication**, which encodes a rotation as well as a scaling. The complex structure changes everything.\n\nThe headline result of this stage:\n\n**Cauchy's theorem.** If $f$ is holomorphic on a simply connected open set $\\Omega$ and $\\gamma$ is a closed contour in $\\Omega$, then\n\n$$\\oint_\\gamma f(z)\\,dz = 0.$$\n\nThis is *the* foundation of complex analysis. Almost everything that follows — Cauchy's integral formula, residues, evaluation of real integrals — is a corollary.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 7,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## A simple example: $\\oint_\\gamma z\\,dz$ around the unit circle [S+N]\n\nParameterise $\\gamma(t) = e^{it}$, $t \\in [0, 2\\pi]$. Then $\\gamma'(t) = i e^{it}$ and\n\n$$\\oint_\\gamma z\\,dz = \\int_0^{2\\pi} e^{it} \\cdot i e^{it}\\,dt = i \\int_0^{2\\pi} e^{2it}\\,dt = 0.$$\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "I1 : integrate(exp(%i*t) * %i * exp(%i*t), t, 0, 2*%pi)$\nprint(\"∮_{|z|=1} z dz =\", I1)$\nprint(\"(Cauchy's theorem says any holomorphic function gives 0 — z is entire ✓)\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 8,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "∮_{|z|=1} z dz = 0\n(Cauchy's theorem says any holomorphic function gives 0 — z is entire ✓)"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## A non-trivial example: $\\oint 1/z\\,dz$\n\nThe function $1/z$ has a singularity at $z = 0$ — it's *not* holomorphic on a disk containing the origin. Cauchy's theorem doesn't apply on a contour enclosing 0.\n\nCompute directly:\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "I2 : integrate(1/exp(%i*t) * %i*exp(%i*t), t, 0, 2*%pi)$\nprint(\"∮_{|z|=1} 1/z dz =\", I2)$\nprint(\"(2πi — the canonical non-zero contour integral)\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 9,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "∮_{|z|=1} 1/z dz = 2*%i*%pi\n(2πi — the canonical non-zero contour integral)"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "$\\oint 1/z\\,dz = 2\\pi i$. This is the foundation of **residue calculus** — the contour integral picks up $2\\pi i$ times the \"residue\" at $z = 0$, which here is just 1.\n\n## Path-independence on simply connected domains\n\nCauchy's theorem has a corollary: on a simply connected $\\Omega$, contour integrals depend only on the endpoints, not the path. So we can define **antiderivatives** $F(z) = \\int_{z_0}^z f(\\zeta)\\,d\\zeta$ unambiguously, and $F'(z) = f(z)$.\n\nThis generalises the fundamental theorem of calculus. Concretely, $\\int_\\gamma z\\,dz = (z_1^2 - z_0^2)/2$ for any path from $z_0$ to $z_1$.\n\nDemonstrate: integrate $z^2$ along two different paths from $0$ to $1 + i$.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Path 1: straight line z(t) = t(1+i), t ∈ [0, 1] */\ngamma1 : t * (1 + %i)$\ngamma1_p : 1 + %i$\nI_path1 : integrate(gamma1^2 * gamma1_p, t, 0, 1)$\nprint(\"∫_path1 z² dz =\", I_path1)$\n\n/* Path 2: along x-axis to 1, then up to 1+i.\n   On segment 1: z = t, t ∈ [0,1]; on segment 2: z = 1 + i*t, t ∈ [0, 1] */\nseg2a : integrate(t^2, t, 0, 1)$\nseg2b : integrate((1 + %i*t)^2 * %i, t, 0, 1)$\nI_path2 : seg2a + seg2b$\nprint(\"∫_path2 z² dz =\", expand(I_path2))$\nprint(\"F(z) = z³/3, F(1+i) - F(0) =\", expand((1 + %i)^3 / 3))$\nprint(\"(All three agree — z² is holomorphic everywhere)\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 10,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "∫_path1 z² dz = (2*%i-2)/3\n∫_path2 z² dz = (2*%i)/3-2/3\nF(z) = z³/3, F(1+i) - F(0) = (2*%i)/3-2/3\n(All three agree — z² is holomorphic everywhere)"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## When path-independence fails: $1/z$ on $\\mathbb{C} \\setminus \\{0\\}$\n\nThe domain $\\mathbb{C} \\setminus \\{0\\}$ is *not* simply connected (a loop around 0 can't be contracted to a point). Path-independence fails: the integral around a closed loop enclosing 0 is $2\\pi i$, not 0.\n\nEquivalently, the \"antiderivative\" $\\int 1/z\\,dz = \\log z$ is multi-valued — $\\log z$ jumps by $2\\pi i$ each time you go around the origin.\n\nVisualise the contour:\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "theta_g : np_to_list(np_linspace(0, 2*float(%pi), 200))$\nunit_re : map(cos, theta_g)$\nunit_im : map(sin, theta_g)$\n\nax_draw2d(\n  color=\"blue\", line_width=2, name=\"contour |z| = 1\",\n  lines(unit_re, unit_im),\n  marker_size=10, color=\"red\", name=\"singularity at z = 0\",\n  points([0.0], [0.0]),\n  title=\"∮_{|z|=1} 1/z dz = 2πi — contour encloses singularity\",\n  xlabel=\"Re z\", ylabel=\"Im z\",\n  grid=true, aspect_ratio=true, showlegend=true\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 11,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
